From 79ddec399e1b8817212154fc4ccb7a71d0db5012 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shiro <build@shiro>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 05:51:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Shiro: a debug agent for gdb: stops, registers, memory,
 breakpoints, steps over a kernel call (1050)

gdb could not start a program: Blink has no ptrace (tabcomputer#108).
Instead of ptrace, the thread that owns the CPU state answers a debugger
itself (blink/shirogdb.inc, docs/research/GDB.md in tabcomputer).

A process host.mjs starts with blink_shiro_gdb(1) keeps its main thread's
`attention` set, so every instruction comes through CheckForSignals and
ShiroGdbTick: breakpoints are addresses checked there (nothing is written
into code, so compiled code needs no invalidation), and the instruction
runs in the interpreter. A stop is a loop of kernel calls (1050): the
thread reports the stop with its registers, the kernel answers with a
command (read or write memory, read or write registers, set or remove a
breakpoint or a watchpoint, continue, step, detach), and the thread
reports the result. A watchpoint is up to 64 bytes of memory compared
after each instruction (8 of them).

Signals become stops. A pending one is shown to the debugger from
ConsumeSignal before it is consumed, which is also where a blocking
kernel call that ended with EINTR looks for it: taken by the debugger,
the call is made again as if no signal had come, so Ctrl-C during a
read doesn't fail the read. A fault an instruction raises is held in
DeliverSignalToUser and reported once HaltMachine has unwound. The
resume command says whether the guest gets the signal. A debugged
thread's tkill/tgkill of itself goes pending rather than being acted on
inside the call, so abort() stops in the debugger first. SIGHUP, SIGINT,
SIGQUIT, SIGUSR1, SIGUSR2, SIGALRM and SIGTERM with the default action
come to the guest rather than the kernel ending the process, so they
reach the debugger.

Only the thread the process started with is debugged; a vfork child on
that thread runs until it execs or exits; fork and exec children are not
debugged. A process started for a debugger that nobody answers (ENOSYS)
turns the agent off and runs.

Without a debugger nothing changes in the instruction loop: the hooks
are one load of g_shiro_gdb in CheckForSignals, ConsumeSignal and
DeliverSignalToUser.
